[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"158","stub":{"id":"158","section":"1) Hwy 20 to the Bear Creek Confluence","altname":"Wilderness Run","river":"Cache Creek","difficulty":"IIstandoutIII","states":["USA-CAL"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"low-runnable","latestReading":{"value":"81","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":"Put in at the BLM parking lot where state Highway 20 crosses the North Fork of Cache Creek. Easy access - Note that the North Fork will be very small compared to Cache Creek.\n\nThere are also significant issues with sweepers and debris in the streambed on this two mile section down to the confluence. We punctured a raft tube here one time on an old trailer axel.","id":"158","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Put-In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":null,"approximate":false},{"type":"rapid","description":"The north fork merges with the main branch of Cache Creek that comes out of Clear Lake after 2 miles. Be sure to note the contrast between the cold clear water of the North Fork (from Snow Mountain via Indian Valley Reservoir) and the warm, smelly, brown water from \"Clear\" Lake.","id":"159","location":{"latitude":"38.98109817504883","longitude":"-122.50399780273438"},"name":"Confluence with Cache Creek","distance":"2","difficulty":null,"approximate":false,"photoID":"0"},{"type":"hazard","description":"At about Mile 4 - distance is approximate. It is in a sharp Right Hand turn.","id":"511","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Ugly Sweeper","distance":"4","photoID":null,"difficulty":null,"approximate":false},{"type":"playspot","description":"Distance approximate - Fun rapid - Left hand turn and drop around large rock. At end of long boulder garden.","id":"512","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Twist and Shout","distance":"8","difficulty":"II+","approximate":false,"photoID":null},{"type":"hazard","description":"This is usually the toughest rapid on the run. It is almost within sight of the take out in a sharp \"S\" turn that starts to the left and then goes back to the right. There is a significant turning drop that flows into a rock/hole (depending on the flow). Scout or portage right.","id":"160","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Mad Mike","distance":"17","photoID":"2099","difficulty":"III","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":"This is a possible take out. It is at the confluence with Bear Creek and it is where you will see Highway 16 and Civilization for the first time in a while. There was an old dump truck abandoned here for many years. It was removed in 2002.","id":"161","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Rusty Truck","distance":"18","photoID":null,"difficulty":null,"approximate":false},{"type":"hazard","description":"This is a good Reason to continue beyond the Rusty Truck take out. There is a II called \"Up Against the Wall\" directly below the Bear Creek Put-in/Take-Out. The next rapid is right on the county line and has a large drop that inevetibly takes you through a hole and shoves you into rocks on river right as there is a 90 degree turn to the left at the bottom of the drop. Fun stuff.","id":"162","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"County Line","distance":"18.5","photoID":null,"difficulty":"III","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"This is often a better take out. It will cost $3 if you leave a car here but you can get very close to the water.","id":"163","location":{"latitude":"38.92190170288086","longitude":"-122.3290023803711"},"name":"Yolo County - Upper Site","distance":"18.8","photoID":"0","difficulty":null,"approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"This is another take out. This is the public campground. It has a nice II+ rapid right below the camp and a great play spot. You can drive to within 100 feet of the river.","id":"164","location":{"latitude":"38.915401458740234","longitude":"-122.31999969482422"},"name":"Yolo County - Middle Site","distance":"20","photoID":"0","difficulty":null,"approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"xU3rk0FrMCaymRByyeE6K","title":"Strainer alert","post_date":"2025-06-11 04:49:05+00","detail":"“We just kayaked the Cache Creek wilderness run today at about 700 cfs on the Rumsey gauge. Just below the put-in on the North Fork of Cache there are a couple of downed trees creating near creek-wide strainers. On most we were able to sneak against the right bank, but it required pretty precise maneuvering. Rafts wouldn’t have made it. It was otherwise a beautiful day out.” Bill Black","user":{"name":"Bethany Overfield"}},{"id":"YvfrRxoP5JEwFUgBrfSdU","title":"Bridge down on NF Cache","post_date":"2025-02-10 17:03:00+00","detail":"Bill Tuthill reported that the BRIDGE, 1/2 way down (~1mile) on the North Fork Cache Creek has partially collapsed. Stay left on any blind corner as it is hard to stop if your on the right when the bridge comes into view. Portage on left.","user":{"name":"Jeff Venturino"}}],"photos":[{"id":"16161","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4754","description":"","caption":"old school enders on Cache Creek, CA","poi_name":"","subject":"Brad Treat enders on Cache Creek","author":"Eddie Gilmartin","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/16161","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/16161"}},{"id":"2098","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2895","description":"William Hodges about to drop into the hole in Mad Mike.  At the usual lower water levels, there is an exposed rock here.","caption":"Mad Mike","poi_name":"Mad Mike","subject":"Willam Hodges","author":"Chuck Bartley","poi_id":"160","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2098","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2098"}},{"id":"2